  "diagnosis": {
    "category": "N_PLUS_ONE",
    "hypothesis": "inventory-service issues one `SELECT ... FROM products WHERE id=$1` per returned row (N+1), amplifying DB round-trips/connection churn against a small pool and causing pool queueing/errors.",
    "root_cause": "inventory-service fetches product details with a per-row query (`SELECT id,name,price,stock FROM products WHERE id=$1`) executed once for every product returned: pg_stat_statements shows 56,920 calls producing 56,920 rows (exactly 1 call per row), versus only 1,683 calls for the batched `SELECT id,name,price,stock FROM products ORDER BY id LIMIT $1`. This N+1 amplification floods the DB with single-row round-trips, each acquiring a pool connection (runtime.query() acquires a connection per statement, pool max_size=4). The inventory pool-stats confirm the resulting saturation: requests_num=60,976 (matching the per-id plus batch calls), requests_queued=490, cumulative requests_wait_ms=294,308, usage_ms=1,027,898, and requests_errors=248 — the signature of many short queries contending for few connections. HTTP latency stayed low (~3ms, 0% error) only because the products table is small, so the amplification degrades pool/DB health rather than individual request latency.",
    "evidence_ids": [
      "E-d08c02695db84f15",
      "E-765c47c7853244e2",
      "E-fe5264e520694240",
      "E-b7f6ea5c788a45dc"
    ],
    "code_locations": [
